  "honest_findings": {
    "pgd": "On PGD the MCU int8 specialists mirror the Edge float-surrogate ordering: chen_query_blinding highest (nsl 0.44/unsw 0.30), then undefended (0.08/0.13), dacm adaptive (0.06/0.04), adv_training ~0, input_smoothing adaptive 0. note dacm_naive>dacm_adaptive on unsw (0.10 vs 0.04) - the rotation defense is cheapest to defeat adaptively, consistent with Edge (midas adaptive 0.06 << naive 0.48).",
    "cw": "C&W-L2 returns defense_success==1.000 across every row - i.e. C&W flips 0 samples on the deployed int8 specialists (both the int8 fake-quant and the continuous dequant replica). This is CONSISTENT with the Edge float surrogate, where undefended C&W already has 0.945 defense success (ASR 0.055). C&W-L2 (BCE-toward-true-label, Adam in tanh space) cannot find low-L2-distance flips on these well-separated classifiers; the initial clamp(0.05,0.95) tanh-anchor creates a transient 17/20 flip by distortion that the optimizer then reverses toward the natural label. The MCU row is therefore not comparable-baseline-meaningful on C&W: it is a degenerate-equal-1.0 row. Report PGD as the discriminating axis and note CW as insensitive on-device.",
    "conclusion": "MCU-tier per-source specialists: on PGD (with Chen rejection counts as defense success), chen_query_blinding is the strongest on-device-feasible baseline (0.44/0.30), and the MIDAS rotation (DACM) adaptive defense-success (0.06/0.04) is low - it is cheaply defeated by an adaptive PGD attacker, mirroring the Edge conclusion. On C&W the int8 specialist is invariant (all 1.000), a degenerate row."
  }
